A||||21||jeffndebrus||Why Bigger Wheels?||||1||
Z||000000||jeffndebrus||11-18-2001||11:54 AM||||ok, I'm new to the arena where everyone tries to remake what it took engineers three years to screw up(kidding) but why does everyone want bigger wheels on their cars?<BR>And how does this effect mpg?<P>just asking||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||11-18-2001||12:00 PM||||Bigger tires. If you go to 17 inch rims you get a tire with a smaller sidewall so basically the overall size of the tire is the same.  I find the less sidewall the "bumper" the ride but then you call it "road feel" and enjoy it because the car handles better now that you have less side wall movement.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadero2dmax||11-18-2001||04:00 PM||||I had this same discussion with "BubbaJoe" about using stock wheels with the Cad motor. His statement was that a 4.x powered Fiero would burn the tires off the stock rims, and suggested going to 16" rims.<P>The circumference of the wheel/tire combo is what determines your performance. I choose to use the stock Fiero rims for my "R" tires because of their lightweight, and chrome MOPAR 5 spoke 14x6.5 wheels for my street tires because of their looks. <P>I personally prefer the 14's because the tires are cheaper - and I use a lot of tires!! Besides, the all wheel, no sidewall tire look is to "RICEY" for me!!<BR> <P>------------------<BR>G<BR>Daily Driver - <BR>85 SE<BR>4.9 Cad<BR>Isuzu 5 speed<BR>'88 cradle<BR>lowered 2 1/2"<BR>Mr. Mikes interior<P>GRM $2002 Challenge Fiero<BR>86 coupe<BR>4.9 (carburated)<BR>2.86:1 TH-125 w/shift kit||||reg||1
Z||000003||filthyscarecrow||11-18-2001||04:19 PM||||the only reason anyone should need wheels bigger than 16" is if yuo're running bigger brakes. sure 17's and 18's ahve better "feel," but the increased weight and rotational inertia will derease acceleration. the threshold fo grip is the same really. i think SCC did a comaprison on this, with a professional driver trying 16, 17, and 18" tires (he didn't know what he was trying- blind test) and while he said the 18's felt the best, he had higher straight speeds with the 16's, as mush as 2mph difference because of the incresed power needed to spin the extra weight. this was on a high HP race car, the difference shuold be even more noticable with less HP. <BR>most people buy the bigger wheels strictly for looks. they do look nice, but the decreased sidewall also gives less protection from potholes. here in MN we have 2 seasons, winter and road construction. the potholes in the spring are the worst i've ever seen (except maybe detroit), so there's no point in goign that big becuase you'll just bend the rims anyway. tires are cheaper for smaller dia. rims. like i said, unless yuo're runnign REALLY big brakes, you don't need big rims...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||11-18-2001||05:19 PM||||it's a compromise for me. i have 16's on my fiero's except one project that may get 17's or 18's. i like the large wheel style but the cost...yeesh. 16's are a little more affordable and give enough style and handling feel to satisfiy me. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||11-18-2001||06:58 PM||||My cast 1-piece 17" wheels are not light, but shod with tyres they ARE lighter than the stock wheel/tyre setup! (15" 6 and 7" on a Formula.)<P>They also handle like a dream, and really made the car stable anywhere above 50mph.<BR>The ride got *smoother* even over bumps, which still puzzles me (other than moving weight to the outside of the circle, rather than in?)<P>That SCC article's 'race car' was a stock Pegout hatchback econobox.  'race car' heh.<P>Grassroots Motorsports did a similar test, this time with just a set of konis on a weenie little Civic.  they tried (stock)14, 15, 16 and 17" wheel/tyre combinations of the same outter rooling diameter.  The crazy 205/40/17s provided the *best* lap times.... <P>(when you have a suspension with increased wheel rates and sophistocation to keep the contact patch on the ground.)<P>Rubber flexing is bad, ummkay? (excepting very tight turning conditions, ie autocross dosen't generally build up any speed, and the turns are so tight, that more rubber flexing around helps to plant the tyres contact patches.  In high-speed racing however, sidewall is the enemy.<P>What matters is what you want your car to be, feel-like, and do.  Pick your setups from there, and ENJOY!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||cadero2dmax||11-18-2001||09:30 PM||||When the 17 and 18 inch wheels cost $1200 and up for the wheels, and the 17 and 18 inch tires are $200 and up each, then that tells me that the crazy Riceburners and coffeecan exhaust has caught up with the "We drive excitement" crowd.<P>I personally will be damned if I will spend $2000 to put tires and wheels on a $3000 or $4000 car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>G||||reg||1
Z||000007||DrDave||11-18-2001||10:19 PM||||     I'm going with 16 inch wheels and 27in. diameter tires for two reasons.  The 16s will clear the brake mods I'm doing,  and the 27 inch diameter tires will give me the same final drive ratio, that the elusive "economy" transmission would've given me.||||reg||1
Z||000008||filthyscarecrow||11-18-2001||10:28 PM||||actually, the SCC article used a rally car. at least the oone i'm thinkin of did. then they tried it in a 200hp focus adn teh difference was even more noticable. in my experience, the bigger sidewall gives more warning when it's about to break away. that's what feels good to me. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||11-18-2001||11:46 PM||||I just blew more on wheels than I did on the car they're going onto, to each his own  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I've driven Fieros with big sidewalls, and little sidwalls.  I like the little ones. Enough to pay for them.<P>Nobody says you have to too. (and you have more money left over for an engine conversion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadero2dmax:<BR><B>When the 17 and 18 inch wheels cost $1200 and up for the wheels, and the 17 and 18 inch tires are $200 and up each, then that tells me that the crazy Riceburners and coffeecan exhaust has caught up with the "We drive excitement" crowd.<P>I personally will be damned if I will spend $2000 to put tires and wheels on a $3000 or $4000 car.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>G</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||1FST2M6||11-19-2001||11:53 AM||||ASTETICS... my 86 SE would handle better on 14s with A032s than it would on my 245 17s. my current GT on 17/18 combo looks much better but fells more responsive just cause there is less slip angle/ sidewall deflection. but mostly astetics. there is no MPG change, no top end or 0-60 change. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstmeat2.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>0-60 in 4.91<P> <A HREF="http://www.1fst2m6.com" TARGET=_blank>www.1fst2m6.com</A>  - underconstruction - but check it out anywho...||||reg||1
Z||000011||Htown86GT||11-19-2001||01:19 PM||||Yes 17's diffinately feel road huggier -<P>------------------<BR>86' GT  17inch Cobras Sus-tec lowered<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://home.swbell.net/ravi1/myfiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||V8Dreamer||11-19-2001||05:09 PM||||2 grand!?!?!?!?!? Maybe if you're really picky, but I only paid $800 for my 17" wheels AND tires.....granted, I could've just paid $300 for new 15" tires, but I wanted both the look and feel of the 17's....they look similar to 1fst2m6's wheels, a silver 5-spoke design...<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex4mula||11-19-2001||05:57 PM||||Mainly looks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> With many new cars coming out of the factory with 16s and 17s the Fiero 15s looks outdated. Our car has an ageless shape. Put 17s or more and many people will think it is a late model.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000014||reddog||11-20-2001||07:03 PM||||Since everyones showing their wheels I couldn't resist. No real advantage to bigger wheels. However, Looks are everything!!<BR>17" StreetRebels<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/04_02A_1.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by reddog (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||IwannaIRM||11-20-2001||07:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadero2dmax:<BR><B>Besides, the all wheel, no sidewall tire look is to "RICEY" for me!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So, is that to say that you think Porsches, Corvettes, Vipers, Firehawks, Camaro SS and the such look "RICEY" since they have 17" and 18" wheels with very low profile performance tires?<P>My Firehawk came with 17" wheels and it doesn't look rice.  I put 17" torque thrust wheels on my 68 Cougar and it doesn't look rice.<P>I guess "RICEY" looking is in the eye of the beholder.<BR><P>------------------<BR>AIM - SWags2933<P>84 Fiero SE (being modified)<BR>88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||11-20-2001||09:52 PM||||Ive got 16 inch with 'tall' 60 series. I do lots of driving on backroads and the way Fieros handle out of the box, I thought it was worth the sacrifice to have a soft ride. Also a taller than stock tire/wheel combo will effectivly change your actual final drive ratio somewhat. You will gain mpg, gain some ground clearance, and top end, but loose a little bit of acceleration. Also throws speedo off, but in mine I changed it to a 200 mph unit anyway.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||onfire||11-21-2001||04:00 AM||||Shop around I bought my wheels and tires for $800. I like the way they look and the way they handle.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bigwheels17.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||cadero2dmax||11-21-2001||07:27 AM||||The original question was  - why does everybody want to go to larger wheels/smaller tires? <P>I have been racing for too many years, I guess. But to me it is all about the drivability and handling of a car.<P>If a car was going straight, it wouldn't need any tires at all, in reality. Might wear away at the wheels - and would not be a comfortable ride - but bare wheels would go straight just fine. Thus, Ben's response about high speeds compared to autocrossing (BTW, and on a tangent, autocrossing is a lot closer to the reality of everyday driving than any other motorsport) is a true statement.<P>It is the turning part where the tires come in to play, and to understand this you need to understand why a slick gives better traction and is faster than a treaded tire. Or, to re-phrase that, you need to know and understand what the tire is doing when it is turning a corner. Each of the four corners is reacting to the turning forces differently. But you need rubber in order for any of the corners to go in any other direction except straight. (Ever try turning a car with 4 flat tires? Well, try that at speed, and you will see what I am saying).<P>Now, if you can understand what the rubber molecules do that no other material can do, then you can fully understand what I am about to say. If you can't understand what rubber and rubber compounds do that makes a car turn, then so be it - I will never win <BR>against the "Duh, I like the way it looks" response.<P>The skinny, no response larger diameter/ smaller sidewall tires are not near as driveable as a larger sidewall performance tire. The larger tire (smaller wheel) with a stiff sidewall will react to the road surface a lot better, because there is simply more rubber to flex and respond. A larger sidewall will give better cornering traction as a result.<P>There are a pair of Fiero racers in Tucson that call their racing team "Team Python". They work for a racing tire distributor so they are able to experiment with various brands and size slicks.  I sold Team Python my old Quad 4 racer with 15x13 rear Hoosier slicks and 13x10 fronts on it. They now run 13's all around - they experimented and found the 13's handled better than the 15's.<P>Somebody, I think it was Iwanna IRM mentioned Corvettes and Vipers and Porsches, etc. Yes, the newer models of these cars have up to 20" wheels. But my friend, you ain't driving one of these cars - with a completely different, newer, far more sophisticated suspension. These suspensions were computer designed to handle with a completely different design - one which the larger wheel combo is part of that design, and made to work in conjuction with. BTW, most of the tires from the cars you mentioned could not even fit the Fiero wheel wells anyway. <P>As an additional point to ponder, I am from the old school that believes - no that should read that KNOWS - adding unsprung weight makes a vehicle handle worse. Most of these 18" wheels weigh a LOT. I think it was Ben that suggested his wheels are ultra heavy, but the wheel/tire combo weigh less than the 15" GT wheel/tire combo. An honest assessment, but what about the 14"? I generally have run a 13 or 14" wheel on Fieros (except the stock Formula I competed in during the 1989-1990 seasons). These wheels give low unsprung weight, and there are still several high performance tires to fit them (I just bought some 206 compound BFG R-1's - the old "Groove-of-Doom stickies - for $47 each new from Tire Rack, how much do your 18" stickies cost?). There are still a few high performance street tires available for the 14" wheel, and at less than $100 per tire. The cost of the 17" amd 18" tires in comparison?<P>Now, not everybody competes with their Fiero, I understand that. Not everybody turns corners, either, I guess. And I admit, some 17" and 18" wheels just flat-out look good on a Fiero. Those looks have both a higher dollar price, and a performance price that is paid. I guess it is a matter if you whether a person is willing to pay that price to look good, or not. <P>I am not. (Of course I don't have a 6" diameter exhaust, either).<P>G||||reg||1
Z||000019||artherd||11-21-2001||09:52 PM||||Yep, though the alloy 15s (and crappy goodyear NON-performance tyres) on my Formula did weigh more than my current 17" (and should weigh more than my other car's eventual 18" setup) I doubt either would be beaten by a set of stamped steel 14" or 13" wheels, with performance tyres.<P>One thing people often forget, is that we must consider the ENTIRE unsprung assembley, as well as the 'partially' sprung components (suspension arms, the springs themselves, shocks, rool bars, axels etc.) in suspension design.<P>Wheel weight means very little unless you couple it with at least tyre weight, and weight of the brake system and hubs/spindle.<P>George raises another very good point, that a low-sidewall tyre is *MUCH* harder to design for, and requires a very very sophistocated suspension to keep the contact patch firmly planted on the ground during all movements of the car...  this is NOT a trivial task, however our cars' suspensions do actually end up doing a fairly decent job, geometry wise.<P>They must be modified from stock in an-off-the-shelf parts way to reain that geometry when pushed however. (busings/springs/bars/shocks/etc) and the non-88 rear at least must GO!   Unlike George, I have not had good success with the non-88 front end, however I have not pushed it half as hard as you have, so I will shut up now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>I perfer my springs to do most the suspending, and the tyres to do most the grabbing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>No 6" exhaust here, just the stock '2 pipes makes 4 pipes' tips here, heh.  <P>I do have a ROAD car that occationally sees competition, but is a street-legal machine designed for driving public roads, thus the goals and compromises are different.<P>I can't tollerate the stock brakes for example. They are inadequate for dissapating the heat of repeated stoping in my expierence (these are on an 88 car, with 'drilled' and dipped rotors, and 'carbonmet' pads.  ATE Super Blue fluid, which I don't *think* I boil... the pads start shooting sparks first  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Therefore, I must go to larger brakes, and the brakes I've actually picked require 18" wheels (some 17s fit, not all.)<P>Yes, the whole shebang ends up being slightly heavier, but far more capable at speed, both on the road, or on the track.<P>High(way) speed stability is also very important in a road car, though not as important in a race-only car. Especially one biased towards slower tracks(road course/autocross hybrid), rather than faster track-only designs.<P>14" wheels don't make for a stable car when put on a Fiero in my expierence.  <P>Lastly, for me the drivability of large-sidewall cars suffers, I just don't personally *like* the way the rubber/air suspends the car, and the resulting undamped oscelations.   That's a personal preference, and yours may infact be entirely 180degrees from mine, and that's great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>One of these days I hope to get together and compare our different styles.<P>Should be fun, untill then, good luck with the GRM 2001 car, I've been following your progress and it looks like it's going very well indeed.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadero2dmax:<BR><B><BR>I have been racing for too many years, I guess. But to me it is all about the drivability and handling of a car.<P>..I think it was Ben that suggested his wheels are ultra heavy, but the wheel/tire combo weigh less than the 15" GT wheel/tire combo. An honest assessment, but what about the 14"? <P>Now, not everybody competes with their Fiero, I understand that. Not everybody turns corners, either, I guess. And I admit, some 17" and 18" wheels just flat-out look good on a Fiero. Those looks have both a higher dollar price, and a performance price that is paid. I guess it is a matter if you whether a person is willing to pay that price to look good, or not. <P>I am not. (Of course I don't have a 6" diameter exhaust, either).<P>G</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000020||lowCG||11-23-2001||12:49 PM||||These cars work better with some sidewall in the front,and rubberbands on the rear,as well as a difference in tire/rim width,if you can find/afford the wheels to do that.<BR>As for the rim size thing,I suppose there's really not much reason,other than the factory alloys weighing more than a steel wheel anyways.<BR>I use a set of 16X7 Enkie racing(17lbs each),and Bridgestone RE71s,205/55,225/50,like it alot,but wish the rear rims could be 8" for a little extra predictability in the rear breakaway,still can't complain much though.||||reg||1
Z||000021||FieroPerformance||11-24-2001||08:32 PM||||I put 16" TSW Blades on my car, I noticed little or no changes in anything other than looks. I like the way they look, that is why I bought them. Make sure you shop around, you can probably find the best prices online from the factory.<P>I got mine for 50% off ($75 per wheel and $79 per tire + $176 shipping). I've had them for about a year now and still love them. The tires that are on there are Toyo, I dont know the number, but they have not worn very much at all.<P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsigfperformance.jpg"> ||||reg||1
